WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:05.877
Every day

00:00:05.919 --> 00:00:09.464
I speak to my beautiful
and brilliant cousin Orna,

00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:11.132
who lives in Israel.

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:16.972
In normal times, we talk
about which cousin is the bigger idiot,

00:00:16.972 --> 00:00:21.142
which honey cake recipe to use,

00:00:21.184 --> 00:00:23.603
which books we're reading,

00:00:23.645 --> 00:00:26.356
the family stories from Belarus --

00:00:26.398 --> 00:00:29.234
this is not Orna,
these are other people in Belarus.

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:32.779
The conversations are a beacon for me,

00:00:32.779 --> 00:00:35.782
and they fill my soul and enter my books.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:43.290
The other day, Orna brought up a Romanian
philosopher named Emil Cioran.

00:00:43.707 --> 00:00:48.503
He was a miserable insomniac

00:00:48.503 --> 00:00:51.464
who drove everyone nuts because of this.

00:00:51.965 --> 00:00:53.133
(Laughter)

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:55.302
That's actually my favorite book of his,

00:00:55.343 --> 00:00:58.722
but the better title,
the more accurate translation,

00:00:58.763 --> 00:01:01.516
is "On the Inconvenience of Being Born."

00:01:01.558 --> 00:01:02.684
(Laughter)

00:01:03.143 --> 00:01:10.025
So he was relentless in talking
about how horrible it was to be alive.

00:01:10.066 --> 00:01:14.487
And he did this until the age of 85,
when he died, which is incredibly ironic.

00:01:14.529 --> 00:01:16.615
(Laughter)

00:01:16.656 --> 00:01:18.408
But I must give him credit.

00:01:18.742 --> 00:01:20.619
He does bring up the essential dilemma:

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:22.996
why are we here, for what purpose?

00:01:22.996 --> 00:01:26.499
But today, I don't really want
to dwell on the morose.

00:01:27.042 --> 00:01:29.002
Let's talk about other things.

00:01:30.086 --> 00:01:31.755
That's not the right slide.

00:01:31.796 --> 00:01:34.215
(Laughter)

00:01:34.215 --> 00:01:35.383
That's the right slide.

00:01:35.425 --> 00:01:36.593
[On death]

00:01:36.593 --> 00:01:39.346
(Laughter)

00:01:40.847 --> 00:01:42.557
So here's Proust.

00:01:44.059 --> 00:01:45.727
Dead, obviously --

00:01:45.769 --> 00:01:47.854
or you think he's sleeping,
but he's dead --

00:01:47.854 --> 00:01:51.399
from a series of paintings
that I’ve done called “Dead in Bed,”

00:01:51.441 --> 00:01:54.778
which includes Tolstoy
and Chekhov, of course.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:56.237
In normal times --

00:01:56.279 --> 00:01:57.947
and these are not normal times,

00:01:57.947 --> 00:02:02.869
these are grim times when the world
is awash in war and killing --

00:02:02.911 --> 00:02:05.372
but in normal times, I have a routine.

00:02:05.955 --> 00:02:10.001
In the early morning hours,
with a strong cup of coffee,

00:02:10.001 --> 00:02:12.128
I read the obituaries.

00:02:12.128 --> 00:02:17.258
The infusion of coffee and biography
affords me a way to reflect.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:22.889
And it might seem too soon in the day
to start with such a tremendous topic.

00:02:22.889 --> 00:02:24.391
But it is a jolt to action

00:02:24.432 --> 00:02:26.059
because it reminds me how fragile

00:02:26.101 --> 00:02:27.936
and how vulnerable we all are,

00:02:27.977 --> 00:02:30.230
and how quickly our lives can end.

00:02:31.106 --> 00:02:32.816
The night is different.

00:02:33.817 --> 00:02:37.237
Then I watch an endless stream
of murder mysteries,

00:02:37.237 --> 00:02:38.863
preferably British.

00:02:39.322 --> 00:02:41.491
Watching them is the kind of solace.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.578
I call this the murder and mint chip
portion of the day.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:49.249
(Laughter)

00:02:49.249 --> 00:02:52.293
We have a problem, we solve the problem.

00:02:52.669 --> 00:02:54.754
People seem briefly upset by the murders,

00:02:54.796 --> 00:02:57.132
so many in every episode.

00:02:57.173 --> 00:02:58.591
But there is no time to brood

00:02:58.591 --> 00:03:00.927
because they have to film
the next episode.

00:03:00.927 --> 00:03:02.971
And they all seem to say,
“Get on with it.”

00:03:03.012 --> 00:03:06.057
And the idea of prevailing over evil

00:03:06.057 --> 00:03:08.768
is my lullaby, and I sleep.

00:03:09.477 --> 00:03:12.605
But what of the day that lies ahead?

00:03:13.022 --> 00:03:15.233
Every one of us invents the day.

00:03:15.275 --> 00:03:17.193
Every single day is invented.

00:03:17.777 --> 00:03:22.490
The actual first day was 13.8
billion years ago, more or less.

00:03:22.782 --> 00:03:25.368
And maybe with the information
from the Webb telescope,

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.829
we can actually see the beginning of time,

00:03:27.871 --> 00:03:30.749
which is an incomprehensible
idea, of course.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:33.501
But what does that perspective afford us

00:03:33.543 --> 00:03:36.671
when contemplating which tutu to wear

00:03:36.713 --> 00:03:39.174
or which insult to respond to

00:03:39.215 --> 00:03:40.925
or what book to write?

00:03:41.509 --> 00:03:47.307
Sometimes the day is too long,
excruciatingly long,

00:03:47.307 --> 00:03:50.059
and I get out of bed,
I look longingly at the bed

00:03:50.101 --> 00:03:51.269
and I say to the bed,

00:03:51.269 --> 00:03:52.437
I will be back soon.

00:03:52.437 --> 00:03:54.147
(Laughter)

00:03:54.189 --> 00:03:57.400
And in between, there are things to know

00:03:57.442 --> 00:03:59.194
and things to not know.

00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:03.740
Here is a map of the United States

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.409
made from memory by my mother, Sarah.

00:04:06.451 --> 00:04:13.416
(Laughter)

00:04:18.546 --> 00:04:22.759
Sarah was the dean
of American history at Harvard.

00:04:22.801 --> 00:04:24.010
(Laughter)

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:25.178
Actually not.

00:04:25.178 --> 00:04:26.221
(Laughter)

00:04:26.221 --> 00:04:31.142
Her family fled the pogroms of Belarus
for Palestine in the 1930s,

00:04:31.184 --> 00:04:34.437
and in 1954 our family
moved to New York City.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:40.109
Her acute sense of the absurd
permeated everything in our life

00:04:40.109 --> 00:04:41.903
in the best way possible.

00:04:42.237 --> 00:04:46.157
This map for me is the gold
standard of knowledge.

00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:49.911
Knowledge as imagination,
knowledge as humor,

00:04:49.953 --> 00:04:51.579
knowledge as not giving a damn

00:04:51.621 --> 00:04:54.332
what the correct answer
is to anything, ever.

00:04:54.374 --> 00:05:01.005
(Laughter and applause)

00:05:02.173 --> 00:05:05.051
Sarah, this is Sarah,
did not speak that much.

00:05:05.051 --> 00:05:08.221
Really, she was of few words,
and we really listened to her.

00:05:08.888 --> 00:05:12.100
And what are the obvious assets
of not speaking?

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.601
So many.

00:05:14.727 --> 00:05:17.689
No worries about repeating
yourself or boring others.

00:05:17.689 --> 00:05:18.690
(Laughter)

00:05:18.731 --> 00:05:22.235
Or boring yourself or being misunderstood.

00:05:22.235 --> 00:05:26.489
No regrets of having said something
inconsiderate or too considerate,

00:05:26.489 --> 00:05:29.117
or too banal or too provocative,

00:05:29.117 --> 00:05:32.829
or just plain stupid
and altogether insufferable.

00:05:33.329 --> 00:05:35.039
That is what happens when we speak,

00:05:35.039 --> 00:05:36.207
it is inevitable.

00:05:36.207 --> 00:05:39.335
No justification at all
for saying much of what we say.

00:05:39.377 --> 00:05:43.882
And if we examine this notion
to its ultimate conclusion,

00:05:43.923 --> 00:05:47.468
obviously the answer is
no more dinner parties.

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:49.637
(Laughter)

00:05:49.637 --> 00:05:51.681
“Wait,” you say, “None at all?

00:05:52.098 --> 00:05:55.018
What if the Tolstoys invite me?”
who are pictured here.

00:05:55.643 --> 00:05:58.396
“Sure, but have you hung out
with the Tolstoys?”

00:05:58.438 --> 00:06:00.857
"Or what if Antony
and Cleopatra invite me?

00:06:00.899 --> 00:06:02.275
Should I attend?"

00:06:02.775 --> 00:06:04.694
“OK,” I say. “But at your own peril.

00:06:04.736 --> 00:06:06.279
You know what happened to them.”

00:06:06.279 --> 00:06:07.697
(Laughter)

00:06:08.656 --> 00:06:10.867
On the other hand,

00:06:10.867 --> 00:06:12.660
if you are invited,

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.080
and let's just say,
it's really lovely to be invited,

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.167
you never know what you're going to see
that will be a painting,

00:06:19.208 --> 00:06:21.127
or hear, that might be a story,

00:06:21.169 --> 00:06:23.671
some fragment, some gesture,

00:06:23.713 --> 00:06:26.382
an image of a dog sitting on a green chair

00:06:26.424 --> 00:06:28.509
in front of radishes and flowers.

00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:33.181
And let's not forget connectedness,
congeniality, conviviality,

00:06:33.181 --> 00:06:35.099
camaraderie,

00:06:35.141 --> 00:06:38.061
bonhomie, merriment, laughter.

00:06:38.102 --> 00:06:39.395
What is wrong with that?

00:06:39.395 --> 00:06:41.731
Nothing, absolutely nothing.

00:06:41.773 --> 00:06:46.152
(Applause)

00:06:46.694 --> 00:06:50.031
I used to be ashamed
of my inconsistencies.

00:06:50.073 --> 00:06:51.658
Now I revel in them.

00:06:52.408 --> 00:06:53.952
Everything is in conflict,

00:06:53.952 --> 00:06:55.828
everything has an opposite,

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:57.914
you are not bound to be one thing,

00:06:57.956 --> 00:07:00.124
a truly liberating notion for me.

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:03.419
You might tell the truth or you might lie.

00:07:03.461 --> 00:07:06.172
You might be kind or unkind,

00:07:06.172 --> 00:07:08.716
selfish or generous, quick or thick.

00:07:08.716 --> 00:07:12.178
And in the midst of that
confusion and that tumult,

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:13.721
the work blossoms.

00:07:14.681 --> 00:07:17.725
To have meaningful work is a salvation,

00:07:17.725 --> 00:07:20.311
and one of my greatest pleasures
is to stare at things,

00:07:20.311 --> 00:07:24.232
and my children always say that I get too
close to people, strangers on the street,

00:07:24.232 --> 00:07:26.234
and just stare at them, at their features,

00:07:26.234 --> 00:07:27.777
because I'm so entranced.

00:07:27.819 --> 00:07:30.446
So I have to be pulled away a little bit.

00:07:30.446 --> 00:07:34.450
But then I can go into my studio
and report on what I have encountered.

00:07:34.826 --> 00:07:38.705
And then, as a bonus,
the bliss of mixing colors,

00:07:38.705 --> 00:07:42.959
of being alone and listening to music
and going into another world.

00:07:43.001 --> 00:07:46.421
Here is a painting from a visit
that I did to Cézanne's studio.

00:07:46.421 --> 00:07:47.964
The walls are painted gray.

00:07:48.464 --> 00:07:51.467
This is the recipe for any
of those who need it:

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:54.429
black, white, ochre and aquamarine.

00:07:54.429 --> 00:07:55.638
Now, as a talisman,

00:07:55.638 --> 00:07:59.142
every painting I do includes Cézanne gray.

00:07:59.934 --> 00:08:02.395
Writing, of course, is different.

00:08:03.229 --> 00:08:05.064
Finding the idea,

00:08:05.106 --> 00:08:06.983
plucking a word out of the air.

00:08:06.983 --> 00:08:08.151
But which word?

00:08:08.151 --> 00:08:10.069
And then a sentence, but which sentence?

00:08:10.111 --> 00:08:12.113
And then a paragraph, and which paragraph?

00:08:12.113 --> 00:08:14.449
And it never stops, so many words.

00:08:14.449 --> 00:08:16.868
I could not live without both
painting and writing,

00:08:16.909 --> 00:08:19.912
and one supports the other
in obvious ways.

00:08:19.912 --> 00:08:22.623
In both, the struggle
is invisible but palpable.

00:08:22.665 --> 00:08:25.752
I say the tears are invisible,
but they're definitely there.

00:08:26.169 --> 00:08:28.337
How do I deal with never good enough?

00:08:28.379 --> 00:08:29.547
Easy.

00:08:29.547 --> 00:08:30.715
[On self-loathing]

00:08:30.757 --> 00:08:33.676
(Laughter)

00:08:34.052 --> 00:08:37.722
“Self-loathing” is a truly
ugly expression,

00:08:37.722 --> 00:08:40.058
but it is an unavoidable condition.

00:08:40.099 --> 00:08:43.269
The crushing anxiety
of not getting it right,

00:08:43.269 --> 00:08:44.979
of not finding the truth,

00:08:45.021 --> 00:08:46.564
of feeling worthless.

00:08:46.606 --> 00:08:47.982
If you look at it one way,

00:08:47.982 --> 00:08:50.443
it could be helpful
to have doubt and dismay.

00:08:50.985 --> 00:08:52.695
Plowing through the muck of despair

00:08:52.695 --> 00:08:56.449
leads to a sense of humanism
and perseverance.

00:08:56.491 --> 00:08:59.660
On the other hand,

00:08:59.702 --> 00:09:03.706
how absurd and tedious to be
in the grips of self-doubt.

00:09:04.332 --> 00:09:07.543
What a waste of time,
that insidious insecurity.

00:09:07.585 --> 00:09:10.379
What of finding your work
and your place on Earth?

00:09:10.421 --> 00:09:12.173
What of self-confidence?

00:09:12.215 --> 00:09:15.551
What of the bliss of finding
the right color or the right word?

00:09:15.593 --> 00:09:16.761
What of sheer joy?

00:09:16.761 --> 00:09:18.221
What is wrong with that?

00:09:18.221 --> 00:09:20.515
Nothing, absolutely nothing.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:24.685
After reading the obituaries,
I go for a walk,

00:09:24.727 --> 00:09:26.104
which is a salvation.

00:09:26.395 --> 00:09:27.605
Here is Robert Walser,

00:09:27.605 --> 00:09:30.108
who wrote one of my favorite
books called "The Walk,"

00:09:30.108 --> 00:09:31.818
which I highly suggest you read.

00:09:31.859 --> 00:09:34.904
Walking and not thinking
is the favorite state.

00:09:34.946 --> 00:09:37.949
An empty brain,
which I call my best brain,

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:42.245
is the only way to have any ideas,
to allow surprises to appear.

00:09:42.578 --> 00:09:46.332
“Solvitur ambulando,” they thought
of this before I did, long ago.

00:09:46.332 --> 00:09:49.919
Walking solves all problems
without trying.

00:09:50.545 --> 00:09:52.713
Walking leads to the pleasure of sitting,

00:09:52.755 --> 00:09:55.174
perhaps on a bus
going down Fifth Avenue,

00:09:55.216 --> 00:09:59.220
and looking at the entire world around you
with a great sense of well-being.

00:09:59.887 --> 00:10:01.639
One day at a farmers market,

00:10:01.639 --> 00:10:05.518
I saw a woman carrying
an absolutely gigantic cabbage.

00:10:05.893 --> 00:10:07.562
(Laughter)

00:10:07.603 --> 00:10:10.481
When I asked to photograph her,
she looked really annoyed.

00:10:10.481 --> 00:10:11.774
(Laughter)

00:10:11.774 --> 00:10:14.819
And for some reason, I was
so delighted by her crankiness.

00:10:14.861 --> 00:10:17.321
It seemed so authentic and true.

00:10:17.363 --> 00:10:19.532
Let's just say what we feel.

00:10:20.241 --> 00:10:22.410
It made me think of all
the things women hold,

00:10:22.451 --> 00:10:24.370
literally and metaphorically.

00:10:24.996 --> 00:10:27.999
Balloons and grudges.

00:10:27.999 --> 00:10:29.000
(Laughter)

00:10:29.041 --> 00:10:32.295
And heavy loads and cabbages.

00:10:33.629 --> 00:10:36.507
And stupendous love and courage.

00:10:38.426 --> 00:10:41.387
And the pink ukulele under a cherry tree.

00:10:41.762 --> 00:10:43.723
And from this a book was formed:

00:10:43.723 --> 00:10:45.349
“Women Holding Things”.

00:10:45.725 --> 00:10:47.226
What do women hold?

00:10:47.852 --> 00:10:49.770
The home and the family

00:10:49.812 --> 00:10:52.064
and the children and the food.

00:10:52.106 --> 00:10:54.275
The friendships, the work,

00:10:54.275 --> 00:10:57.028
the work of the world
and the work of being human,

00:10:57.028 --> 00:11:00.156
the memories and the troubles
and the sorrows

00:11:00.198 --> 00:11:02.074
and the triumphs and the love.

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:05.411
Men do as well,
but not quite in the same way.

00:11:06.495 --> 00:11:10.416
Sometimes when I'm feeling
particularly happy or content,

00:11:10.458 --> 00:11:14.629
I think I can provide sustenance
for legions of human beings.

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:17.506
I can hold the entire world in my arms.

00:11:17.548 --> 00:11:20.134
Other times, I can barely cross the room.

00:11:20.176 --> 00:11:22.511
And I drop my arms, frozen.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:25.306
There is never an end to holding,

00:11:25.348 --> 00:11:28.726
and certainly there is often
the feeling of never doing enough.

00:11:29.101 --> 00:11:32.230
And then there is the next day
and the next day.

00:11:32.688 --> 00:11:34.023
And one holds on.

00:11:35.358 --> 00:11:37.276
I dreamt

00:11:37.276 --> 00:11:39.737
someone else was stupid for a change.

00:11:39.779 --> 00:11:40.988
(Laughter)

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:42.281
Such a relief.

00:11:42.323 --> 00:11:43.741
(Laughter)

00:11:43.741 --> 00:11:45.451
Albeit a fleeting one.

00:11:48.246 --> 00:11:49.413
A few years ago,

00:11:49.455 --> 00:11:50.831
I did an illustrated edition

00:11:50.831 --> 00:11:53.125
of "The Autobiography of Alice B. Toklas,"

00:11:53.167 --> 00:11:55.878
written by the inimitable Gertrude Stein.

00:11:56.671 --> 00:11:59.215
Alice and Gertrude lived a singular life,

00:11:59.257 --> 00:12:03.302
one of intense creativity
and equally intense domesticity,

00:12:03.302 --> 00:12:05.554
and one could not exist without the other.

00:12:06.305 --> 00:12:10.393
That, to me, is the ideal of life,
family and work.

00:12:10.935 --> 00:12:16.023
My son Alex and I made a short film
called "My Name is Alice B. Toklas,"

00:12:16.065 --> 00:12:17.775
and for a brief day

00:12:17.775 --> 00:12:21.612
I became Alice with two
extra noses on my nose.

00:12:22.321 --> 00:12:25.616
Here is a short clip
from the end of the film.

00:12:25.658 --> 00:12:27.326
[Picasso was an Idiot]

00:12:27.368 --> 00:12:29.036
(Piano music in background)

00:12:32.415 --> 00:12:35.001
(Video) It took Picasso 90 sittings

00:12:35.042 --> 00:12:37.670
to paint Gertrude Stein's portrait.

00:12:38.087 --> 00:12:39.880
Nobody thought it looked like her,

00:12:39.922 --> 00:12:42.341
but he said, "Don't worry, it will."

00:12:44.635 --> 00:12:48.306
But actually, I don't know if he knew
anything about what he was talking about

00:12:48.306 --> 00:12:51.309
oh, you know, Picasso, he was impossible.

00:12:53.352 --> 00:12:55.521
(Laughter)

00:13:11.579 --> 00:13:12.830
Thank you very much.

00:13:17.376 --> 00:13:19.003
Oh, this looks delicious.

00:13:20.546 --> 00:13:22.548
Absolutely delicious.

00:13:39.899 --> 00:13:42.401
(Laughter)

00:14:00.795 --> 00:14:02.213
(Piano music ends)

00:14:03.214 --> 00:14:08.844
(Applause)

00:14:08.844 --> 00:14:11.347
The hot dog was delicious,

00:14:11.389 --> 00:14:14.809
and dancing down Fifth Avenue
was a complete joy.

00:14:15.142 --> 00:14:16.977
And as we say in our family,

00:14:16.977 --> 00:14:21.273
doing almost everything
in the strangeness of life

00:14:21.273 --> 00:14:23.734
through the sorrows of life, live.

00:14:23.776 --> 00:14:24.985
Thank you very much.

00:14:24.985 --> 00:14:28.239
(Applause and cheers)

